# Accessibility

A heavily animated site fails quietly for assistive tech unless each effect is
explicitly accounted for. These are the recurring cases in this codebase.

## Split text

The parent carries the full sentence; the fragments are hidden.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — announced as a run of disconnected words
<h2>{words.map((w) => <motion.span key={w}>{w}</motion.span>)}</h2>

// ✅ GOOD
<motion.h2 aria-label={text}>
  {words.map((w, i) => (
    <span key={i} aria-hidden="true" className="inline-flex overflow-hidden">
      <motion.span variants={item}>{w}&nbsp;</motion.span>
    </span>
  ))}
</motion.h2>
```

## Decorative elements

Marquees, oversized ghost numerals and watermark wordmarks get `aria-hidden="true"`.
They repeat content that is already in the accessible tree.

## Tabs and carousels

Tabbed UI uses the real pattern: `role="tablist"` / `role="tab"` with `aria-selected`
and `aria-controls`, roving `tabIndex`, and arrow-key navigation. See `sections/stack.tsx`.

## Motion preferences

Pinned and horizontal-scroll sections must degrade to a normally scrollable layout.
Guard on both mount and preference so the first render matches SSR:

```tsx
const pinned = mounted && !reduce && distance > 0;
```

## Baseline

Icon-only controls need `aria-label`. Status messages need `role="status"` and
`aria-live="polite"`. Never remove the focus outline — it is styled globally.
